course: dictionary
lesson: 7652
title: Who makes the promise?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A promiser is a person who makes a promise. A promise is a firm word that you will do something. The promiser is the one doing the promising, not the one who receives the promise. Context helps you spot this: «a good promiser keeps promises» tells you the promiser is the person who makes them.
explain: Look at the words around the word. «A great promiser» may make many promises and keep few. The clue is keep few: a promiser who keeps few promises is not very trustworthy, even if they promise a lot. Always ask whether the sentence is about the person who promises or the person who is promised something.
explain: Try the swap test. Put «the person who makes a promise» in place of promiser. If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: A promiser is a person who makes a promise.
fact: «A great promiser» may make many promises and keep few.
fact: In law, the promiser is the person in an agreement who promises to do something. It is also spelled «promisor».
fact: The promiser has a duty to the person they promise something to, so keeping promises matters.
fact: «Promiser» is a rare word, so you will mostly meet it in stories, ethics lessons and law.
exercise: In «the promiser should explain honestly», who is the promiser? | The person who receives the promise | *The person who makes the promise | The weather | A bird that sings
exercise: «A great promiser» may make many promises. What does this suggest? | *Makes lots of promises but keeps few | Never makes any promises | Keeps every promise they make | Is always late
exercise: In «the promiser should explain honestly», copy the word that names the person who makes the promise. | explain | *promiser | should | honestly
test: In law, what else is the promiser called? | *promisor | promisee | promised | promising
test: Which word describes a good promiser? | *Someone who keeps their promises | Someone who never speaks | Someone who makes no promises at all | Someone who breaks every promise
exam: Which sentence uses promiser correctly? | The promiser is the room where we eat. | The promiser is the rain falling on the roof. | The promiser is a cold, wet day. | *Mia is the promiser when she says, «I will help you tomorrow.»
